<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # All About the Weather – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Perfect Day!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Count up to 50 out loud with help. Interactive: Describe simple pictures or objects during classroom discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can count weather-related items up to 50 with assistance (e.g., counting "clouds" or "raindrops" in a picture). Can describe simple weather-related pictures or objects during classroom discussions (e.g., "This is a rainy day." "The umbrella is red."). Can respond to peer questions about weather and objects using simple phrases (e.g., "What is this?" "It's an umbrella.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification to activate prior knowledge and introduce the lesson's focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Weather Guessing Game." The teacher shows large flashcards of different weather conditions (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) one by one. Students call out the weather name chorally. The teacher asks, "What is the weather like today?" and guides students to respond: "It's a [sunny/rainy/cloudy/windy] day!" This reinforces vocabulary from previous lessons and focuses on oral production.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of target sounds and phrases. The teacher says, "It's a <b>s</b>unny day!" emphasizing the /s/ sound. Then, "It's a <b>r</b>ainy day!" emphasizing the /r/ sound. Next, the teacher shows a flashcard of a windy day and says, "It's a <b>w</b>indy day!" paying attention to the initial /w/ and final /i/ sounds. Students repeat chorally after each model.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and simple questions to check understanding.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a sunny day) "Is it hot or cold?" (Expected: "Hot!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a rainy day) "Do I need an umbrella?" (Expected: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About the Weather."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, colorful picture story or comic strip titled "My Perfect Day!" showing different children enjoying various weather conditions. (e.g., a child playing outside on a sunny day, a child wearing a coat and boots on a rainy day, a child flying a kite on a windy day, a child building a snowman on a snowy day - though snowy might be out of context for Panama, replace with "cold" or "cloudy" activities). Genre: Simple narrative. Register: Informal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing the weather and activities: "It's a sunny day, and I play outside." Responding to questions: "What's the weather like?" "It's rainy. I need my umbrella." Expressing preferences: Talking about preferences related to weather (e.g., "I like sunny days.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher shows each picture from the story and asks simple questions, prompting short oral responses.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look at the picture! What is the weather like?" (Students respond: "It's sunny!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does the boy do when it's sunny?" (Students respond: "Play outside!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does she like rainy days?" (Students respond: "Yes!" or "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is this?" (pointing to an umbrella or boots in the picture) (Students respond: "It's an umbrella!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking through controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Adjectives + nouns ("rainy day"), Present simple ("I like sunny days."), WH-questions ("What is the weather like?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> sun, rain, raindrop, coat, hat, day, cloud, wind, storm, umbrella, boots, scarf, puddle, rainbow, sunny, rainy, warm, cold, cloudy, windy, play, wear, like.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /s/ in "sunny", /r/ in "rainy", initial /w/ and final /i/ in "windy".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Describing the weather and activities, responding to questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Expressing preferences.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Weather Item Chant (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher displays large flashcards of weather conditions and associated items (e.g., sunny day, sun; rainy day, umbrella; windy day, scarf). The teacher points to a card and prompts: "It's a [sunny] day. I see the [sun]!" Students repeat chorally. The teacher ensures clear pronunciation of /s/ in "sunny" and "sun", and /r/ in "rainy". They repeat with different cards, reinforcing "adjective + noun" structures. The teacher also asks "What is this?" pointing to an item, students reply "It's an umbrella."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"I Like" Sentences (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill):</b> The teacher holds up various weather flashcards. For each card, the teacher asks, "What is the weather like?" Students respond, "It's a [sunny] day." Then, the teacher prompts, "What do you like?" Students say, "I like [sunny] days!" or "I like to [play] outside." The teacher models and guides students to use different verbs and adjectives, including "windy" (emphasizing /w/ and /i/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Picture Count and Describe (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a "weather picture" with many items (e.g., a rainy day with many raindrops, clouds, puddles, or a sunny day with many flowers, birds, suns). Students take turns asking: "What is the weather like?" and responding: "It's a [rainy] day." Then, they point to items and count together out loud up to 50: "How many raindrops? One, two, three, four... (up to 50) raindrops!" They also describe items: "The umbrella is red." The teacher circulates, listens, and provides support.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Perfect Weather Day!" Show & Tell.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity:</b> Students draw a picture of "A Perfect Day!" for them, including the weather and what they would do. They can draw sunny days with children playing outside, rainy days with children jumping in puddles, or windy days with kites. They also draw some items to count (e.g., clouds, flowers, raindrops). In small groups (3-4 students), each student takes turns presenting their drawing and describing it to their peers.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This is my perfect day! It's a [sunny] day. I like [sunny] days. I [play] outside, and I see [many] clouds."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They then point to an item in their drawing and say, "I have [five] clouds. One, two, three, four, five!" (or up to 50, if their drawing allows for more items to count).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Peers ask simple questions: "What is this?" (pointing to an item) or "Do you like [rainy] days?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, encourages interaction, and provides prompts if needed.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Perfect Weather Day!" Show & Tell. The checklist will focus on student's ability to speak about the weather and count.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student briefly presents their "Perfect Day" drawing to the teacher (or to the whole class if time permits) and describes the weather and one activity, and counts 5-10 items in their picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /s/, /r/, /w/, /i/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (weather adjectives like "sunny," "rainy," nouns like "umbrella," "cloud," verbs like "like," "play").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of simple sentences (e.g., "It's a sunny day," "I like sunny days.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to count weather-related items (e.g., "three clouds," "ten raindrops") up to 50 with assistance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to simple peer or teacher questions using short phrases.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by circling a happy face or a neutral face next to two statements:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say what the weather is like." (Happy face / Neutral face)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can count things in a picture." (Happy face / Neutral face)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite weather word to say today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to talk about today? What was a little difficult?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What speaking activities worked best to encourage oral production and interaction among students?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to use the target grammar (adjectives + nouns, present simple, WH-questions) and vocabulary effectively in their descriptions and interactions?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the counting task (up to 50) appropriate, and what adjustments might be needed for more support or challenge?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I further encourage clearer pronunciation of /s/, /r/, /w/, and /i/ in the next lesson?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the vocabulary and sentence structures practiced today to write simple sentences and short paragraphs about the weather and their preferences, building on their oral descriptions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>